    "['hello-interval [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ldp discovery hello interval 1', 'parent_command': ['mpls ldp advertise-labels interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'mpls ldp advertise-labels interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent for the Juniper LDP hello-interval configuration:\\n\\n##\\nmpls ldp discovery hello interval 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, the hello interval is set globally for all interfaces under LDP, unlike Juniper where it can be set per-interface. The Cisco command applies to both ge-1/0/0.0 and ge-2/0/0.0 interfaces (or their Cisco equivalents GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0).\", 'trans_template': 'mpls ldp discovery hello interval 1'}]]": 1,
    "['hold-time [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ldp discovery hello holdtime 5', 'parent_command': ['mpls ldp advertise-labels interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'mpls ldp advertise-labels interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent for the Juniper LDP interface hold-time configuration:\\n\\n##\\nmpls ldp discovery hello holdtime 5\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, the LDP hold-time is configured globally for all interfaces (not per-interface like Juniper). The command sets the hold time for both link and targeted hello messages. If you need different hold times for different interfaces in Cisco, you would need to use interface-specific commands with different syntax.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls ldp discovery hello holdtime 5'}]]": 1,
    "['hello-interval [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ldp discovery hello interval 1', 'parent_command': ['mpls ldp advertise-labels interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'mpls ldp advertise-labels interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the LDP hello interval settings:\\n\\n##\\nmpls ldp discovery hello interval 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, the LDP hello interval is configured globally (applies to all interfaces) rather than per-interface like in Juniper. The command sets the hello interval to 1 second for all LDP interfaces. If you need interface-specific settings in Cisco, you would use \"mpls ldp discovery hello holdtime\" under the interface configuration, but this would require additional context about the specific interface configuration.', 'trans_template': 'mpls ldp discovery hello interval 1'}]]": 1,
    "['hold-time [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ldp discovery hello holdtime 5', 'parent_command': ['mpls ldp advertise-labels interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'mpls ldp advertise-labels interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the Juniper LDP interface hold-time settings:\\n\\n##\\nmpls ldp discovery hello holdtime 5\\nmpls ldp discovery hello interval 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, the hold-time and hello-interval are configured globally for all LDP interfaces (not per-interface like Juniper)\\n2. The values are specified in seconds (same as Juniper)\\n3. Both commands are needed to fully represent the Juniper configuration since hello-interval and hold-time work together\\n4. The interface-specific settings in Juniper would be handled by enabling LDP on the interfaces separately in Cisco with \"mpls ip\"', 'trans_template': 'mpls ldp discovery hello holdtime 5'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ldp discovery hello interval 1', 'parent_command': ['mpls ldp advertise-labels interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'mpls ldp advertise-labels interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco configuration for the Juniper LDP interface hold-time settings:\\n\\n##\\nmpls ldp discovery hello holdtime 5\\nmpls ldp discovery hello interval 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, the hold-time and hello-interval are configured globally for all LDP interfaces (not per-interface like Juniper)\\n2. The values are specified in seconds (same as Juniper)\\n3. Both commands are needed to fully represent the Juniper configuration since hello-interval and hold-time work together\\n4. The interface-specific settings in Juniper would be handled by enabling LDP on the interfaces separately in Cisco with \"mpls ip\"', 'trans_template': 'mpls ldp discovery hello interval 1'}]]": 1,
